              <span>Chapter 02</span> &bull; <span
                >Security &amp; Cryptography</span
              >
            </div>
            <h3
              class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white font-sans"
            >
              2. Cryptographic Signature Verification: HMAC-SHA256, Ed25519
              &amp; Content-Digest
            </h3>
            <p>
              Because webhook receiver endpoints must be publicly accessible on
              the open internet, security is paramount. Without robust
              authentication, malicious actors can send forged HTTP POST
              requests containing fabricated payment receipts, unauthorized user
              permissions, or corrupted datasets. To guarantee authenticity and
              data integrity, premier providers utilize <strong
                >Hash-based Message Authentication Codes (HMAC)</strong
              > or <strong>Asymmetric Public-Key Cryptography (Ed25519)</strong
              >.
            </p>
            <p>
              The fundamental principle of HMAC signature verification requires
              computing a keyed cryptographic hash (such as <code
                >HMAC-SHA256</code
              >) over the <em>exact, unmodified raw request body bytes</em> concatenated
              with a delivery timestamp, using a shared signing secret. The resulting
              hexadecimal or base64 digest is then compared against the provider's
              signature header (e.g. <code>Stripe-Signature</code>, <code
                >X-Hub-Signature-256</code
              >, <code>X-Shopify-Hmac-Sha256</code>, <code
                >X-Razorpay-Signature</code
              >).
            </p>

            <div class="space-y-3 pt-2">
              <h4
                class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white font-sans"
              >
                Solving the Notorious "No signatures found matching the expected
                signature for payload" Error
              </h4>
              <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">
                The most frequent bug encountered in webhook development is
                signature verification failure. In over 95% of incidents, this
                error occurs because the backend web framework (such as Express <code
                  >express.json()</code
                >, Next.js App Router, or Fastify) automatically parsed the raw
                UTF-8 byte stream into a JavaScript object before signature
                computation. Re-serializing a JavaScript object back to JSON via <code
                  >JSON.stringify()</code
                > alters whitespace, key ordering, and character escapes, permanently
                invalidating the HMAC digest.
              </p>
              <pre
                class="bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl text-xs overflow-x-auto font-mono"><code set:text={signatureCodeSample} /></pre>
            </div>
          </section>

