            </div>
            <h3
              class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white font-sans"
            >
              3. Webhook Latency Simulation, Status Code Debugging &amp;
              Exponential Backoff Retries
            </h3>
            <p>
              In production environments, network conditions are never ideal.
              Upstream webhook providers enforce strict connection timeouts: <strong
                >Stripe enforces a 10-second timeout</strong
              >, <strong>GitHub enforces 10 seconds</strong>, <strong
                >Shopify enforces 5 seconds</strong
              >, and <strong>Slack enforces a rigorous 3-second deadline</strong
              >. If your receiving server performs long-running synchronous
              database queries, PDF generation, or third-party API dispatches
              during the HTTP request cycle, your endpoint will exceed the
              timeout deadline, triggering a <code>504 Gateway Timeout</code> or <code
                >502 Bad Gateway</code
              > failure.
            </p>
            <p>
              SafeWebhook incorporates an integrated <strong
                >Simulated Latency Engine (0–5000ms delay slider)</strong
              > and <strong
                >Custom Response Status Code Simulator (200, 201, 204, 400, 401,
                429, 500, 504)</strong
              >. This enables engineering teams to rigorously test upstream
              provider retry behavior, circuit breakers, and exponential backoff
              algorithms without writing custom mock infrastructure.
            </p>

